<node id="11128833" title="Re^5: Dereferencing in blessed object" created="2021-02-26 08:22:07" updated="2021-02-26 08:22:07" _doctext_mode="strict"><type id="11">note</type>
<author id="830549">haukex</author>
<doctext field_name="doctext">&lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;i&gt;can you immediately tell if $$x{key} means ${$x}{key} or ${$x{key}} ?&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Personally I've just memorized this special case and yes, when I see &lt;c&gt;$$x{key}&lt;/c&gt; or &lt;c&gt;$$y[2]&lt;/c&gt; I just know this means &lt;c&gt;$x-&gt;{key}&lt;/c&gt; or &lt;c&gt;$y-&gt;[2]&lt;/c&gt;. I sometimes find it a bit nicer because of the parallels bewteen e.g. &lt;c&gt;my %x = ( key =&gt; 42 ); print $x{key};&lt;/c&gt; and &lt;c&gt;my $x = { key =&gt; 42 }; print $$x{key}&lt;/c&gt;, but I also completely understand that people prefer &lt;c&gt;$x-&gt;{key}&lt;/c&gt;, and I often use this style myself, for example I prefer &lt;c&gt;$self-&gt;{key}&lt;/c&gt; over &lt;c&gt;$$self{key}&lt;/c&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
</doctext>
